EKS Cluster & AWS Load Balancer Controller Setup Guide
1. Prerequisites & Infrastructure

1.1. Cluster IAM Role: AmazonEKSAutoClusterRole

Service: EKS

Attached Policies:

AmazonEKSBlockStoragePolicy, AmazonEKSClusterPolicy, AmazonEKSComputePolicy, AmazonEKSLoadBalancingPolicy, AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy

Trust Relationship:

JSON
{
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [
        {
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Principal": { "Service": "eks.amazonaws.com" },
            "Action": ["sts:AssumeRole", "sts:TagSession"]
        }
    ]
}
1.2. Node IAM Role: AmazonEKSNodeRole

Service: EC2

Attached Policies:

AmazonEC2ContainerRegistryPullOnly, AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy, AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy

Trust Relationship: Standard ec2.amazonaws.com assume role.

1.3. Networking (VPC & Subnets)

VPC: CIDR 10.10.0.0/16 (Enable DNS Hostnames & Support).

Public Subnets (A, B, C): * Mandatory Tags: * kubernetes.io/role/elb = 1
* kubernetes.io/cluster/<cluster-name> = shared

Settings: Enable "Auto-assign public IPv4 address".

Connectivity: Create Internet Gateway (IGW) and update Route Table (0.0.0.0/0 -> IGW).

2. Cluster Deployment & IAM Integration

2.1. Deployment Steps

Create EKS Cluster (Auto Mode or Standard).

Crucial: Associate IAM OIDC Provider.

Install Add-ons: VPC CNI, CoreDNS, kube-proxy.

Create Node Group using the Role from step 1.2.

2.2. Controller IAM Role: AmazonEKSLoadBalancerControllerRole

This role allows the Kubernetes pod to talk to AWS.

Policies: AWSLoadBalancerControllerIAMPolicy, AmazonEC2FullAccess.

Trust Relationship: > Note: Every time you create a new cluster, you MUST update the OIDC ID in this Trust Relationship to match the new cluster's OIDC provider URL.

JSON
{
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [
        {
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Principal": {
                "Federated": "arn:aws:iam::<ACCOUNT_ID>:oidc-provider/<OIDC_URL>"
            },
            "Action": "sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity",
            "Condition": {
                "StringEquals": {
                    "<OIDC_URL>:aud": "sts.amazonaws.com",
                    "<OIDC_URL>:sub": "system:serviceaccount:kube-system:aws-load-balancer-controller"
                }
            }
        }
    ]
}
3. Ubuntu Configuration & Connection

Update Config: aws eks update-kubeconfig --region us-east-1 --name <cluster-name>

Access Entry: If you get Forbidden, go to EKS Console -> Access -> Create Access Entry. Add your IAM User and associate AmazonEKSClusterAdminPolicy.

4. Kubernetes Configuration (The "Golden Manifest" Run)

4.1. Cert-Manager Installation

Install the certificate manager first. If you encounter OpenAPI validation errors, use the --validate=false flag.

Bash
kubectl apply -f https://github.com/cert-manager/cert-manager/releases/download/v1.14.3/cert-manager.yaml --validate=false

# Wait for all pods to be 1/1 Running
kubectl get pods -n cert-manager
4.2. Ingress Controller Deployment

Open your ingress-controller-v4.yaml (Golden Manifest) in Sublime.

Update: Ensure --cluster-name and --aws-vpc-id match your new environment.

Apply Manifest:

Bash
kubectl apply -f ingress-controller-v4.yaml
4.3. Verification

Bash
# Verify Controller is Running (Check for 1/1 status)
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l app.kubernetes.io/name=aws-load-balancer-controller

# Deploy Test App & Ingress
kubectl apply -f test-ingress.yaml

# Check if Load Balancer DNS is generated
kubectl get ingress
Troubleshooting Tips for Tomorrow:

Pod in Pending? Run kubectl describe pod <name> -n kube-system. Check for "Insufficient CPU" or "untolerated taints".

ALB not appearing in AWS? Check controller logs: kubectl logs -n kube-system -l app.kubernetes.io/name=aws-load-balancer-controller. Look for AccessDenied (IAM/OIDC issue) or subnets not found (Tagging issue).

Certificates Pending? Restart the injector: kubectl rollout restart deployment cert-manager-cainjector -n cert-manager.
